BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School
DTSTART:20260928T160000Z
DTEND:20260928T173000Z
LOCATION:The Ostrom Workshop\, 513 N PARK AVE
GEO:39.171057;-86.524959
SUMMARY:Ostrom Workshop | Colloquium: Vaishnavi Tripuraneni
DESCRIPTION:Bio  Vaishnavi Tripuraneni (she/her) is an Assistant Professo
 r in the department of Geography and Spatial Sciences at the University o
 f Delaware (UD). She is a political ecologist whose work draws on framewo
 rks from agrarian political economy\, human geography\, feminist approach
 es to people-environment interactions\, and human-dimensions of environme
 ntal change. Using mixed methods\, she has conducted extensive research i
 n south India on agrarian debt and biodiversity conservation in human-man
 aged landscapes\, human dimensions of invasive plant management in Califo
 rnia\, and climate resilience in Delaware.  Prior to joining UD\, she was
  a postdoctoral scholar in the Human Dimensions Lab\, Purdue University. 
 She has a Ph.D. in Environmental Studies from the University of Wisconsin
 -Madison. At UD\, Tripuraneni serves as Director of the Environmental Stu
 dies undergraduate program. She teaches human geography and environmental
  studies courses and applies community-engaged teaching and learning peda
 gogy to provide students real-world experience while addressing local\, p
 lace-based\, issues identified by the community.Title  Life and Debt: pro
 duction and reproduction in agrarian south IndiaAbstract  Debt\, a fundam
 ental part of agrarian life and a key contributor to agrarian change\, un
 dermines farmer autonomy\, limits future farm investments\, and sets the 
 emotional and economic conditions of households. Farmers juggle debts to 
 meet not just agricultural needs but also everyday household reproductive
  expenses\, from medical bills to marriage expenses\, in ways not well un
 derstood. This research explores the relationship between productive and 
 reproductive debt in rural Telangana\, India. Life and debt (oral) histor
 ies and surveys of farmers capture experiences of indebtedness and strugg
 les to repay loans. The analysis shows that: 1) reproductive debt is a si
 gnificant part of overall debt\; 2) productive and reproductive debt are 
 intertwined in complicated ways\; and 3) these entanglements lead to casc
 ading patterns of debt\, including borrowing to repay previous loans. The
 se together point to the financialization of reproduction in the agrarian
  household\, with implications for lending\, social service provision\, a
 nd smallholder survival.About the Colloquium SeriesEach Monday throughout
  the academic year\, speakers share and discuss cutting edge research ech
 oing the themes of the Ostrom Workshop's research program. Topics range f
 rom the governance of space\, cybersecurity\, and data management\, to th
 e environment\, political economy\, and sustainable food science. Present
 ers are from IU and around the world. Join us at the workshop or over Zoo
 m to learn how the Ostrom legacy is evolving today. For more information\
 , see ourwebsite (https://ostromworkshop.indiana.edu/index.html).
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong><span data-contrast="none"><pictu
 re class="lw_image lw_image31395  lw_align_left lw_block"><source type="i
 mage/webp" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/105/width/300/hei
 ght/300/31395_headshot-2021-tripuraneni.rev.1782501567.webp 1x" data-orig
 in="responsive"/><source type="image/jpeg" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/
 live/image/gid/105/width/300/height/300/31395_headshot-2021-tripuraneni.r
 ev.1782501567.jpg 1x" data-origin="responsive"/><img width="300" height="
 300" alt="" data-alt="" data-decoration="true" src="https://events.iu.edu
 /live/image/gid/105/width/300/height/300/31395_headshot-2021-tripuraneni.
 rev.1782501567.jpg" data-max-w="590" data-max-h="590" loading="lazy" data
 -optimized="true"/></picture></span></strong></p><p><strong><span data-co
 ntrast="none">Bio</span></strong></p><p>  Vaishnavi Tripuraneni (she/her)
  is an Assistant Professor in the department of Geography and Spatial Sci
 ences at the University of Delaware (UD). She is a political ecologist wh
 ose work draws on frameworks from agrarian political economy\, human geog
 raphy\, feminist approaches to people-environment interactions\, and huma
 n-dimensions of environmental change. Using mixed methods\, she has condu
 cted extensive research in south India on agrarian debt and biodiversity 
 conservation in human-managed landscapes\, human dimensions of invasive p
 lant management in California\, and climate resilience in Delaware.</p><p
 >  Prior to joining UD\, she was a postdoctoral scholar in the Human Dime
 nsions Lab\, Purdue University. She has a Ph.D. in Environmental Studies 
 from the University of Wisconsin-Madison. At UD\, Tripuraneni serves as D
 irector of the Environmental Studies undergraduate program. She teaches h
 uman geography and environmental studies courses and applies community-en
 gaged teaching and learning pedagogy to provide students real-world exper
 ience while addressing local\, place-based\, issues identified by the com
 munity.</p><p><strong><span data-contrast="none">Title</span></strong></p
 ><p>  Life and Debt: production and reproduction in agrarian south India<
 /p><p><strong>Abstract</strong></p><p>  Debt\, a fundamental part of agra
 rian life and a key contributor to agrarian change\, undermines farmer au
 tonomy\, limits future farm investments\, and sets the emotional and econ
 omic conditions of households. Farmers juggle debts to meet not just agri
 cultural needs but also everyday household reproductive expenses\, from m
 edical bills to marriage expenses\, in ways not well understood. This res
 earch explores the relationship between productive and reproductive debt 
 in rural Telangana\, India. Life and debt (oral) histories and surveys of
  farmers capture experiences of indebtedness and struggles to repay loans
 . The analysis shows that: 1) reproductive debt is a significant part of 
 overall debt\; 2) productive and reproductive debt are intertwined in com
 plicated ways\; and 3) these entanglements lead to cascading patterns of 
 debt\, including borrowing to repay previous loans. These together point 
 to the financialization of reproduction in the agrarian household\, with 
 implications for lending\, social service provision\, and smallholder sur
 vival.</p><p><strong><span data-contrast="none">About the Colloquium Seri
 es</span></strong></p><p><span data-contrast="none">Each Monday throughou
 t the academic year\, speakers share and discuss cutting edge research ec
 hoing the themes of the Ostrom Workshop's research program. Topics range 
 from the governance of space\, cybersecurity\, and data management\, to t
 he environment\, political economy\, and sustainable food science. Presen
 ters are from IU and around the world. Join us at the workshop or over Zo
 om to learn how the Ostrom legacy is evolving today. For more information
 \, see our</span><span data-contrast="none"><a class="external" href="htt
 ps://ostromworkshop.indiana.edu/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener
 ">website</a></span><span data-contrast="none">.</span></p>
