BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Hamilton Lugar School
DTSTART:20260930T170000Z
DTEND:20260930T180000Z
LOCATION:The Ostrom Workshop\, 513 N PARK AVE
GEO:39.171057;-86.524959
SUMMARY:Ostrom Workshop | Research Series: Anasthascia Boateng
DESCRIPTION:Bio  Anasthascia Boateng is a fourth-year Ph.D. student in th
 e Department of Anthropology. Her research examines how African internati
 onal students in the United States navigate transnational care\, resource
  sharing\, and family obligations within diasporic communities. Drawing o
 n economic anthropology\, migration studies\, and diaspora philanthropy\,
  she examines the concept of "Black Tax\," the expectation that individua
 ls who achieve educational or economic mobility provide support to family
  and community members across borders. As an Ostrom research awardee and 
 fellow\, she investigates how informal institutions\, collective action\,
  and shared obligations shape students' everyday lives and contribute to 
 broader conversations about governance\, reciprocity\, and social support
  in transnational settings.Title  "Black Tax": Diaspora Philanthropy or B
 urden? Governing Transnational Care among African International StudentsA
 bstract  This paper examines how African international students in the Un
 ited States navigate the financial\, emotional\, and social obligations a
 ssociated with Black tax the expectation that upwardly mobile individuals
  will provide material and social support to family members and communiti
 es across borders. Drawing on in-depth interviews with my interlocutors\,
  the study investigates whether remittance is experienced as diaspora phi
 lanthropy\, moral responsibility\, collective care\, or personal burden a
 nd under what conditions these meanings shift. The findings reveal that r
 emittances operate simultaneously as expressions of love\, kinship obliga
 tion\, and collective investment\, while also requiring students to reduc
 e personal consumption\, defer savings\, and make difficult trade-offs be
 tween transnational responsibilities and daily life in the United States.
 About the Ostrom Workshop Research Series  Learn about innovative researc
 h by Graduate Students and Visiting Scholars as they advance the mission 
 of the Ostrom Workshop\, building upon the theme of governance to underst
 and and address major societal problems.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><picture class="lw_image lw_image31981  l
 w_align_left lw_block"><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://events.i
 u.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/src_region/
 0\,94\,1320\,1413/31981_ABoateng.rev.1786724464.webp 2x\, https://events.
 iu.edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/src_region
 /0\,94\,1320\,1413/31981_ABoateng.rev.1786724464.webp 3x" data-origin="re
 sponsive"/><source type="image/jpeg" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/i
 mage/scale/2x/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/src_region/0\,94\,1320\
 ,1413/31981_ABoateng.rev.1786724464.jpg 2x\, https://events.iu.edu/live/i
 mage/scale/3x/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/src_region/0\,94\,1320\
 ,1413/31981_ABoateng.rev.1786724464.jpg 3x" data-origin="responsive"/><im
 g width="300" height="300" alt="" data-alt="" data-decoration="true" src=
 "https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/src
 _region/0\,94\,1320\,1413/31981_ABoateng.rev.1786724464.jpg" srcset="http
 s://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1
 /src_region/0\,94\,1320\,1413/31981_ABoateng.rev.1786724464.jpg 2x\, http
 s://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1
 /src_region/0\,94\,1320\,1413/31981_ABoateng.rev.1786724464.jpg 3x" data-
 max-w="1320" data-max-h="1319" loading="lazy" data-optimized="true"/></pi
 cture></p><p><strong>Bio</strong></p><p>  Anasthascia Boateng is a fourth
 -year Ph.D. student in the Department of Anthropology. Her research exami
 nes how African international students in the United States navigate tran
 snational care\, resource sharing\, and family obligations within diaspor
 ic communities. Drawing on economic anthropology\, migration studies\, an
 d diaspora philanthropy\, she examines the concept of "Black Tax\," the e
 xpectation that individuals who achieve educational or economic mobility 
 provide support to family and community members across borders. As an Ost
 rom research awardee and fellow\, she investigates how informal instituti
 ons\, collective action\, and shared obligations shape students' everyday
  lives and contribute to broader conversations about governance\, recipro
 city\, and social support in transnational settings.</p><p><strong>Title<
 /strong></p><p>  "Black Tax": Diaspora Philanthropy or Burden? Governing 
 Transnational Care among African International Students</p><p><strong>Abs
 tract</strong></p><p>  This paper examines how African international stud
 ents in the United States navigate the financial\, emotional\, and social
  obligations associated with Black tax the expectation that upwardly mobi
 le individuals will provide material and social support to family members
  and communities across borders. Drawing on in-depth interviews with my i
 nterlocutors\, the study investigates whether remittance is experienced a
 s diaspora philanthropy\, moral responsibility\, collective care\, or per
 sonal burden and under what conditions these meanings shift. The findings
  reveal that remittances operate simultaneously as expressions of love\, 
 kinship obligation\, and collective investment\, while also requiring stu
 dents to reduce personal consumption\, defer savings\, and make difficult
  trade-offs between transnational responsibilities and daily life in the 
 United States.</p><p><strong>About the Ostrom Workshop Research Series</s
 trong></p><p>  Learn about innovative research by Graduate Students and V
 isiting Scholars as they advance the mission of the Ostrom Workshop\, bui
 lding upon the theme of governance to understand and address major societ
 al problems.</p>
