SUMMARY:Ostrom Workshop | Colloquium: Andrew Flachs
DESCRIPTION:Bio  Dr. Andrew Flachs researches food and agriculture system
 s\, exploring genetically modified crops\, heirloom seeds\, and our own m
 icrobiomes. An associate professor of anthropology at Purdue University\,
  his work among farmers in North America\, the Balkans\, and South India 
 investigates ecological knowledge and technological change in agricultura
 l systems. Funded by institutions including the National Geographic Socie
 ty\, the American Institute of Indian Studies\, the National Science Foun
 dation\, and the Social Science Research Council\, Andrew's work has resu
 lted in over three dozen peer-reviewed publications\, five books\, and pu
 blic-facing work in venues including Sapiens\, Civil Eats\, Salon\, and t
 he National Geographic magazine. Outside of academia\, he is an avid cook
 \, cyclist\, embarrassing dad to three children\, and musician who has pe
 rformed in New York City\, St. Louis\, Asheville\, and the San Francisco 
 Bay Area.Title  Agriculture as social reproduction: Identifying mutually 
 reinforcing diversity in small farmsAbstract  Social reproduction theory 
 illuminates how the material and social fabrics of society are formed\, r
 ecognizing value that is otherwise externalized in capitalist accounting.
  While it offers a robust vocabulary for describing how class\, identity\
 , and other ways of being in society are formed\, this approach rarely pl
 aces lived experiences within larger political ecologies. In this talk\, 
 I consider both how social reproduction reproduces socioeconomic conditio
 ns and class relations\, as well as how this work reproduces physical lan
 dscapes that generate future possibilities for organizing life. Agricultu
 re is a particularly illuminating case to describe how work reproduces so
 cial relationships while continually creating a landscape in which divers
 e life can thrive (or suffer). Across India\, Bosnia\, and the US Midwest
 \, three different sorts of farms with three very different regional hist
 ories\, I explore the role of commons and public infrastructure in creati
 ng diverse ecologies that sustain diverse social relations that sustain d
 iverse economies.About the Colloquium SeriesEach Monday throughout the ac
 ademic year\, speakers share and discuss cutting edge research echoing th
 e themes of the Ostrom Workshop's research program. Topics range from the
  governance of space\, cybersecurity\, and data management\, to the envir
 onment\, political economy\, and sustainable food science. Presenters are
  from IU and around the world. Join us at the workshop or over Zoom to le
 arn how the Ostrom legacy is evolving today. For more information\, see o
 urwebsite (https://ostromworkshop.indiana.edu/index.html).
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong><span data-contrast="none"><pictu
