 " loading="lazy" data-optimized="true"/></picture></span></strong></p><p>
 <strong><span data-contrast="none">Bio</span></strong></p><p>  Dr. Andrew
  Flachs researches food and agriculture systems\, exploring genetically m
 odified crops\, heirloom seeds\, and our own microbiomes. An associate pr
 ofessor of anthropology at Purdue University\, his work among farmers in 
 North America\, the Balkans\, and South India investigates ecological kno
 wledge and technological change in agricultural systems. Funded by instit
 utions including the National Geographic Society\, the American Institute
  of Indian Studies\, the National Science Foundation\, and the Social Sci
 ence Research Council\, Andrew's work has resulted in over three dozen pe
 er-reviewed publications\, five books\, and public-facing work in venues 
 including Sapiens\, Civil Eats\, Salon\, and the National Geographic maga
 zine. Outside of academia\, he is an avid cook\, cyclist\, embarrassing d
 ad to three children\, and musician who has performed in New York City\, 
 St. Louis\, Asheville\, and the San Francisco Bay Area.</p><p><strong>Tit
 le</strong></p><p>  Agriculture as social reproduction: Identifying mutua
 lly reinforcing diversity in small farms</p><p><strong>Abstract</strong><
 /p><p>  Social reproduction theory illuminates how the material and socia
 l fabrics of society are formed\, recognizing value that is otherwise ext
 ernalized in capitalist accounting. While it offers a robust vocabulary f
 or describing how class\, identity\, and other ways of being in society a
 re formed\, this approach rarely places lived experiences within larger p
 olitical ecologies. In this talk\, I consider both how social reproductio
 n reproduces socioeconomic conditions and class relations\, as well as ho
 w this work reproduces physical landscapes that generate future possibili
 ties for organizing life. Agriculture is a particularly illuminating case
  to describe how work reproduces social relationships while continually c
 reating a landscape in which diverse life can thrive (or suffer). Across 
 India\, Bosnia\, and the US Midwest\, three different sorts of farms with
  three very different regional histories\, I explore the role of commons 
 and public infrastructure in creating diverse ecologies that sustain dive
 rse social relations that sustain diverse economies.</p><p><strong><span 
 data-contrast="none">About the Colloquium Series</span></strong></p><p><s
 pan data-contrast="none">Each Monday throughout the academic year\, speak
 ers share and discuss cutting edge research echoing the themes of the Ost
 rom Workshop's research program. Topics range from the governance of spac
 e\, cybersecurity\, and data management\, to the environment\, political 
 economy\, and sustainable food science. Presenters are from IU and around
  the world. Join us at the workshop or over Zoom to learn how the Ostrom 
 legacy is evolving today. For more information\, see our</span><span data
 -contrast="none"><a class="external" href="https://ostromworkshop.indiana
 .edu/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">website</a></span><span d
 ata-contrast="none">.</span></p>
