LOCATION:IU CINEMA
GEO:39.168613;-86.517376
SUMMARY:Mangrove School + Makongo/Caterpillars w/Q&A | For All the Children
DESCRIPTION:Mangrove School(Filipa César and Sónia Vaz Borges\, Guinea 
 Bissau\, 2022): During the anticolonial struggle against Portuguese occup
 ation\, the free schools in Guinea Bissau moved deep into the jungle and 
 found the mangroves. The schools were fashioned on the interwoven branche
 s and roots. The directors film the mangroves as an infrastructure with a
  networked architecture\, as the locus of transmission\, of radical educa
 tion\, attentive to the memory of the struggles. The mangrove's branches 
 are powerful and encourage networks\, collectives\, and horizontality. [3
 5 min\; documentary\; Portuguese with English subtitles]Makongo/Caterpill
 ars(Elvis Sabin Ngaïbino\, Central African Republic\, 2020): Albert and 
 André strive to create a school in their forest community in equatorial 
 Central African Republic. As the only secondary school graduates in their
  village\, they teach local children while relying on the annual makongo 
 (caterpillar) harvest to fund their project. This vivid ethnographic port
 rait offers no embellishment\, no narration\, and no added music. The dir
 ect cinema approach is remarkably sensitive\, attuned to the sounds of an
 imals\, the rain forest\, and the daily life of the men and their familie
 s. [73 min\; documentary\; Sango and French with English subtitles]A Q&am
 p\;A will follow the screening.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><i><strong>Mangrove School</strong></i><s
 trong>(Filipa César and Sónia Vaz Borges\, Guinea Bissau\, 2022):</stro
 ng> During the anticolonial struggle against Portuguese occupation\, the 
 free schools in Guinea Bissau moved deep into the jungle and found the ma
 ngroves. The schools were fashioned on the interwoven branches and roots.
  The directors film the mangroves as an infrastructure with a networked a
 rchitecture\, as the locus of transmission\, of radical education\, atten
 tive to the memory of the struggles. The mangrove's branches are powerful
  and encourage networks\, collectives\, and horizontality. [35 min\; docu
 mentary\; Portuguese with English subtitles]</p><p><i><strong>Makongo</st
 rong></i><strong>/</strong><i><strong>Caterpillars</strong></i><strong>(E
 lvis Sabin Ngaïbino\, Central African Republic\, 2020):</strong> Albert 
 and André strive to create a school in their forest community in equator
 ial Central African Republic. As the only secondary school graduates in t
 heir village\, they teach local children while relying on the annual mako
 ngo (caterpillar) harvest to fund their project. This vivid ethnographic 
 portrait offers no embellishment\, no narration\, and no added music. The
  direct cinema approach is remarkably sensitive\, attuned to the sounds o
 f animals\, the rain forest\, and the daily life of the men and their fam
 ilies. [73 min\; documentary\; Sango and French with English subtitles]</
 p><p><i><strong>A Q&amp\;A will follow the screening.</strong></i></p>
