LOCATION:The Ostrom Workshop\, 513 N PARK AVE
GEO:39.171057;-86.524959
SUMMARY:Ostrom Workshop | Research Series: Kyuwoong Kyeong
DESCRIPTION:Bio  Kyuwoong Kyeong is a Ph.D. candidate at the O'Neill Scho
 ol of Public and Environmental Affairs at Indiana University Bloomington.
  His research explores how public employees and citizens respond to compl
 ex administrative contexts in which tensions arise among rules\, discreti
 on\, and public values. His research interests include behavioral public 
 administration\, public sector decision-making\, prosocial behavior\, and
  citizen evaluations of government. Focusing on prosocial rule-breaking a
 nd whistleblowing\, his dissertation examines how the meaning and consequ
 ences of these behaviors for citizens and public organizations vary acros
 s administrative and institutional contexts.Title  Expecting Cold Decisio
 ns\, but Desiring Warm Bureaucrats? Evidence from Two Survey Experiments 
 on U.S. CitizensAbstract  Prosocial rule-breaking (PSRB) by public employ
 ees—the intentional violation of formal rules to help others—embodies
  a fundamental tension between rule adherence and responsiveness to citiz
 ens' welfare. Although prior research has largely examined why bureaucrat
 s engage in PSRB\, less is known about how citizens respond when public e
 mployees break rules for prosocial purposes. This research draws on two p
 reregistered survey experiments with U.S. adults to examine how citizens 
 evaluate both rule-breaking decisions and the bureaucrats who make them\,
  and how these evaluations vary across contexts. The first experiment foc
 uses on the client\, testing whether need and prior effort shape reaction
 s to a bureaucrat's rule-breaking decision. The second focuses on the bur
 eaucrat\, examining whether competence and consistency in prior decisions
  alter those reactions. Across the two studies\, citizens' evaluations ar
 e highly context-dependent. In the first experiment\, PSRB reduces approv
 al of the decision and perceptions of the bureaucrat's integrity and comp
 etence while simultaneously increasing perceived benevolence and willingn
 ess to be served by the bureaucrat. Client need also makes several evalua
 tions of the rule-breaking bureaucrat more favorable. In the second exper
 iment\, however\, PSRB increases approval of the decision\, overall trust
 worthiness\, and willingness to be served\, while continuing to increase 
 perceived benevolence and reduce perceived integrity. These favorable res
 ponses are stronger when the bureaucrat is highly competent. Together\, t
 he findings show that citizens' responses to bureaucratic rule-breaking d
 epend on characteristics of both the beneficiary and the bureaucrat\, as 
 well as on whether citizens are evaluating the decision itself or the bur
 eaucrat who made it.About the Ostrom Workshop Research Series  Learn abou
 t innovative research by Graduate Students and Visiting Scholars as they 
 advance the mission of the Ostrom Workshop\, building upon the theme of g
 overnance to understand and address major societal problems.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><picture class="lw_image lw_image31982  l
 w_align_left lw_block"><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://events.i
 u.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/src_region/
 0\,25\,2133\,2156/31982_Photo_Kyeong.rev.1786725055.webp 2x\, https://eve
 nts.iu.edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/src_re
 gion/0\,25\,2133\,2156/31982_Photo_Kyeong.rev.1786725055.webp 3x" data-or
 igin="responsive"/><source type="image/jpeg" srcset="https://events.iu.ed
 u/live/image/scale/2x/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/src_region/0\,2
 5\,2133\,2156/31982_Photo_Kyeong.rev.1786725055.jpg 2x\, https://events.i
 u.edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/src_region/
 0\,25\,2133\,2156/31982_Photo_Kyeong.rev.1786725055.jpg 3x" data-origin="
 responsive"/><img width="300" height="300" alt="" data-alt="" data-decora
 tion="true" src="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/105/width/300/heigh
 t/300/crop/1/src_region/0\,25\,2133\,2156/31982_Photo_Kyeong.rev.17867250
 55.jpg" title="Photo Kyeong" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/sca
 le/2x/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/src_region/0\,25\,2133\,2156/31
 982_Photo_Kyeong.rev.1786725055.jpg 2x\, https://events.iu.edu/live/image
 /scale/3x/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/src_region/0\,25\,2133\,215
 6/31982_Photo_Kyeong.rev.1786725055.jpg 3x" data-max-w="2133" data-max-h=
 "2131" loading="lazy" data-optimized="true"/></picture></p><p><strong>Bio
 </strong></p><p>  Kyuwoong Kyeong is a Ph.D. candidate at the O'Neill Sch
 ool of Public and Environmental Affairs at Indiana University Bloomington
 . His research explores how public employees and citizens respond to comp
 lex administrative contexts in which tensions arise among rules\, discret
 ion\, and public values. His research interests include behavioral public
  administration\, public sector decision-making\, prosocial behavior\, an
 d citizen evaluations of government. Focusing on prosocial rule-breaking 
 and whistleblowing\, his dissertation examines how the meaning and conseq
 uences of these behaviors for citizens and public organizations vary acro
 ss administrative and institutional contexts.</p><p><strong>Title</strong
 ></p><p>  Expecting Cold Decisions\, but Desiring Warm Bureaucrats? Evide
 nce from Two Survey Experiments on U.S. Citizens</p><p><strong>Abstract</
 strong></p><p>  Prosocial rule-breaking (PSRB) by public employees—the 
 intentional violation of formal rules to help others—embodies a fundame
 ntal tension between rule adherence and responsiveness to citizens' welfa
 re. Although prior research has largely examined why bureaucrats engage i
 n PSRB\, less is known about how citizens respond when public employees b
 reak rules for prosocial purposes. This research draws on two preregister
 ed survey experiments with U.S. adults to examine how citizens evaluate b
 oth rule-breaking decisions and the bureaucrats who make them\, and how t
 hese evaluations vary across contexts. The first experiment focuses on th
 e client\, testing whether need and prior effort shape reactions to a bur
 eaucrat's rule-breaking decision. The second focuses on the bureaucrat\, 
 examining whether competence and consistency in prior decisions alter tho
 se reactions. Across the two studies\, citizens' evaluations are highly c
 ontext-dependent. In the first experiment\, PSRB reduces approval of the 
 decision and perceptions of the bureaucrat's integrity and competence whi
 le simultaneously increasing perceived benevolence and willingness to be 
 served by the bureaucrat. Client need also makes several evaluations of t
 he rule-breaking bureaucrat more favorable. In the second experiment\, ho
 wever\, PSRB increases approval of the decision\, overall trustworthiness
 \, and willingness to be served\, while continuing to increase perceived 
 benevolence and reduce perceived integrity. These favorable responses are
  stronger when the bureaucrat is highly competent. Together\, the finding
 s show that citizens' responses to bureaucratic rule-breaking depend on c
 haracteristics of both the beneficiary and the bureaucrat\, as well as on
  whether citizens are evaluating the decision itself or the bureaucrat wh
 o made it.</p><p><strong>About the Ostrom Workshop Research Series</stron
 g></p><p>  Learn about innovative research by Graduate Students and Visit
 ing Scholars as they advance the mission of the Ostrom Workshop\, buildin
 g upon the theme of governance to understand and address major societal p
 roblems.</p>
