SUMMARY:Korean Rice Cake Workshop
DESCRIPTION:Thursday\, October 8thAvailable sessions:&#160\;  Session 1: 
 11:00 - 12:30 p.m.  Session 2: 2:00 - 3:30 p.m. FULL - all new registered
  participants will be put on the waitlistAt GISB 1060&#160\;355 N Eagleso
 n Av.  Bloomington 47405  &#160\;  This event is free and open to the pub
 lic.&#160\;  All materials will be provided.&#160\;  RSVP ONLY  &#160\;Ab
 out Songpyeon:  Songpyeon (송편) is a traditional Korean treat made of 
 glutinous rice. It is a type of tteok (떡)\, small rice cakes\, traditio
 nally eaten during the Korean autumn harvest festival\, Chuseok (추석).
  Songpyeon symbolizes a full moon and was used for ancestral celebrations
  along with other foods to show appreciation for the year's harvest. Kore
 ans hoped and prayed to help them avoid misfortune.About&#160\;Jennifer L
 ee Ban:Jennifer Lee Ban\, also known as Mama Ban\, is the founder of Rice
  Blossoms\, a modern Korean dessert brand rooted in tradition and wellnes
 s. After becoming a new mom\, she struggled to find wholesome snacks for 
 her daughters while seeking a creative outlet after stepping away from he
 r career as a professional interior designer. Her search led her to the a
 rt of Korean dessert-making\, where she discovered beautifully crafted tr
 eats that were both nourishing and deeply meaningful. Mama Ban was drawn 
 to traditional Korean rice cakes for their natural qualities—they are g
 luten-free\, often vegan\, and thoughtfully made. Today\, through Rice Bl
 ossoms\, she reimagines these time-honored desserts with a modern sensibi
 lity\, steaming each one with care and intention.&#160\;  You will be abl
 e to take your Songpyeon home!  Light refreshments will be served.&#160\;
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Thursday\, October 8th<br /></str
 ong>Available sessions:&#160\;<br />  Session 1: 11:00 - 12:30 p.m.<br />
   Session 2: 2:00 - 3:30 p.m. <strong>FULL</strong> - all new registered 
 participants will be put on the waitlist</p><p><strong>At GISB 1060&#160\
 ;<br /></strong>355 N Eagleson Av.<br />  Bloomington 47405</p><p style="
 text-align: center\;">  &#160\;</p><p style="text-align: center\;">  This
  event is free and open to the public.&#160\;<br />  All materials will b
 e provided.&#160\;<strong><br /><br />  RSVP ONLY</strong></p><p>  &#160\
 ;</p><p><strong>About Songpyeon:</strong><br />  Songpyeon (송편) is a 
 traditional Korean treat made of glutinous rice. It is a type of tteok (�
 ��)\, small rice cakes\, traditionally eaten during the Korean autumn har
 vest festival\, Chuseok (추석). Songpyeon symbolizes a full moon and wa
 s used for ancestral celebrations along with other foods to show apprecia
 tion for the year's harvest. Koreans hoped and prayed to help them avoid 
 misfortune.</p><p><strong>About&#160\;</strong><strong>Jennifer Lee Ban:<
 br /></strong>Jennifer Lee Ban\, also known as Mama Ban\, is the founder 
 of Rice Blossoms\, a modern Korean dessert brand rooted in tradition and 
 wellness. After becoming a new mom\, she struggled to find wholesome snac
 ks for her daughters while seeking a creative outlet after stepping away 
 from her career as a professional interior designer. Her search led her t
 o the art of Korean dessert-making\, where she discovered beautifully cra
 fted treats that were both nourishing and deeply meaningful. Mama Ban was
  drawn to traditional Korean rice cakes for their natural qualities—the
 y are gluten-free\, often vegan\, and thoughtfully made. Today\, through 
 Rice Blossoms\, she reimagines these time-honored desserts with a modern 
 sensibility\, steaming each one with care and intention.&#160\;</p><p sty
 le="text-align: center\;"><br />  You will be able to take your Songpyeon
  home!<br />  Light refreshments will be served.&#160\;</p>
