SUMMARY:Byrnes Institute Colloquium: Madeline Stull\, "Makeshift Modernit
 y: The (In)Formal Urbanization of Interwar Belgrade"
DESCRIPTION:This talk examines the (in)formal dynamics of Belgrade's urba
 nization during the interwar period\, focusing on the parallel developmen
 t of residential and municipal infrastructure in two adjacent neighborhoo
 ds: Jatagan-mala\, the city's largest informal settlement\, and Kotež Ne
 imar\, a privately developed neighborhood. Challenging narratives that ma
 rginalize the urban poor as peripheral to the city's development\, Stull 
 argues that informal settlement and formal planning were mutually constit
 utive processes. Drawing on archival documents\, urban plans\, and contem
 porary reporting\, she shows how a regulatory and property-centered moder
 nizing logic structured Belgrade's growth through displacement and dispos
 session. Municipal anxieties about the city's form and order materialized
  in the streets\, producing hierarchies of urban belonging and practices 
 of ownership grounded in bourgeois respectability\, with violent conseque
 nces for the city's majority-poor population.  Madeline Stull is a Ph.D. 
 candidate in East European History (with minors in Cultural History and D
 igital Humanities) at Indiana University\, Bloomington. She earned a Mast
 er's and Bachelor of Arts in European History from Arizona State Universi
 ty and Barrett\, the Honor's College in 2020 and 2021 respectively. Her r
 esearch has been supported by the U.S. Fulbright Commission and an Americ
 an Councils research fellowship. Madeline's most recent publication\, "Th
 e Fantasy of Reason: Reevaluating Belgrade's 20th Century Urban Plans thr
 ough Borislav Pekić's Houses\," is available in Historical Geography iss
 ue no. 52. She is currently working on a dissertation entitled\, Makeshif
 t Modernity: The Creation of Belgrade at the Intersection of (In)Formalit
 y\, which historizes and spatializes the marginalization of Belgrade's ur
 ban Others to better understand the genealogy of modern urban management 
 practices in a newly formed\, peripheral nation.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  This talk examines the (in)formal dynam
 ics of Belgrade's urbanization during the interwar period\, focusing on t
 he parallel development of residential and municipal infrastructure in tw
 o adjacent neighborhoods: Jatagan-mala\, the city's largest informal sett
 lement\, and Kotež Neimar\, a privately developed neighborhood. Challeng
 ing narratives that marginalize the urban poor as peripheral to the city'
 s development\, Stull argues that informal settlement and formal planning
  were mutually constitutive processes. Drawing on archival documents\, ur
 ban plans\, and contemporary reporting\, she shows how a regulatory and p
 roperty-centered modernizing logic structured Belgrade's growth through d
 isplacement and dispossession. Municipal anxieties about the city's form 
 and order materialized in the streets\, producing hierarchies of urban be
 longing and practices of ownership grounded in bourgeois respectability\,
  with violent consequences for the city's majority-poor population.</p><p
 >  Madeline Stull is a Ph.D. candidate in East European History (with min
 ors in Cultural History and Digital Humanities) at Indiana University\, B
 loomington. She earned a Master's and Bachelor of Arts in European Histor
 y from Arizona State University and Barrett\, the Honor's College in 2020
  and 2021 respectively. Her research has been supported by the U.S. Fulbr
 ight Commission and an American Councils research fellowship. Madeline's 
 most recent publication\, "The Fantasy of Reason: Reevaluating Belgrade's
  20th Century Urban Plans through Borislav Pekić's Houses\," is availabl
 e in Historical Geography issue no. 52. She is currently working on a dis
 sertation entitled\, Makeshift Modernity: The Creation of Belgrade at the
  Intersection of (In)Formality\, which historizes and spatializes the mar
 ginalization of Belgrade's urban Others to better understand the genealog
 y of modern urban management practices in a newly formed\, peripheral nat
 ion.</p>
